\[E_c = 5000\sqrt{f_{ck}} \quad \text{(IS 456 Cl 6.2.3.1)}\] <p>This tells you how stiff the concrete is — how much it deforms under load.</p> <p>For M25: \(E_c = 5000 \times \sqrt{25} = 5000 \times 5 = 25{,}000\) N/mm²</p> <p>Compare this to steel: \(E_s = 200{,}000\) N/mm². Steel is 8 times stiffer than M25 concrete. This is why steel carries disproportionately more stress for the same strain — a fact that drives the entire theory of RC design.</p> <p>Think of it like... two springs in parallel. The stiffer spring (steel) takes more of the force. If you pull two ropes tied together — one rubber (concrete) and one steel wire — the steel wire takes most of the load because it deforms less per unit force.</p> <p>Our library computes this: <pre><code># From Python/structural_lib/codes/is456/materials.py
def get_ec(fck: float) -&gt; float:
    """Modulus of Elasticity of Concrete (IS 456 Cl. 6.2.3.1)"""
    return 5000 * math.sqrt(fck)
</code></pre></p>

